## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence clearly establishes that Robin Vos did **not** lose his seat in the 2018 election. He won re-election in Assembly District 63, defeating Democrat Joel Jacobsen. While Republicans did lose one seat in the Assembly in 2018, that was the 14th District seat held by Rep. Dale Kooyenga, who chose to run for State Senate rather than seek re-election—making it an open seat flip, not a defeat of an incumbent. Ballotpedia explicitly confirms that "no incumbents were defeated in the general election" in 2018. The claim conflates Vos personally losing his seat with the broader Republican caucus losing a seat, which is a factual misrepresentation.

## Missing Information

No additional information is needed. The evidence is clear and comprehensive enough to reach a definitive conclusion.

## Conclusion

The claim is **refuted**. Robin Vos did not lose his seat in the 2018 election—he won re-election. The one Republican-held Assembly seat that flipped to Democratic control in 2018 was an open seat (the 14th District, vacated by Dale Kooyenga), and no Republican incumbents were defeated in that election cycle. The claim fundamentally misrepresents what occurred.

